|---|---|---|
| Stars | 0 | 10 |
| Maintainer | Single individual (`dfa1`), no org | "Premo-Cloud" org — **zero public members**, one repo total |
| Age | First commit ~Sep 20, 2026 | First commit ~Sep 19, 2026 |
| Maven Central | Referenced in README, but my direct search against `search.maven.org` returned zero matches | Same — claimed in README, unconfirmed by direct search |
| Affiliation | Unofficial | Explicitly states: "not affiliated with, endorsed by, or supported by TypeSafe AI" |

The bigger fact underneath both: **Jev itself only launched around September 15, 2026** — two weeks ago. That means both "community SDKs" are wrapping a two-week-old API, are themselves one to two weeks old, and have had essentially no time for anyone to find bugs, security issues, or bad API-key handling in them. The Premo-Cloud "organization" in particular has the shape of a shell (one repo, no visible members) rather than a real maintaining entity — that's the one I'd trust least of the two.

**Why direct HTTP is the better call here, not just the cautious one:** from what we already learned about the API shape — a single `POST /v1/systemone`, Bearer-token auth, a JSON body with named `Choice`/`Score`/`Noul` questions, typed JSON back — this is a thin enough surface that calling it directly with Java's built-in `HttpClient` plus the Jackson dependency the project already has (`LlmJudgeScorer` already parses JSON) is barely more code than wiring up an external SDK. For that little savings, pulling in an unaudited, zero-track-record dependency that will hold your API key isn't worth it — especially for code you're using in front of a live webinar audience, where "it broke because of a two-week-old dependency's bug" is a bad failure mode to debug on stage.

